rcent approval rating for  donald trump in india now that's  certainly far higher than he would see  in any european country or at home he  could only wish for  numbers like that in the united states  running into this election but there's  it's not just the personal relationship  between these two men and all the  commonalities they share there's also  some real strategic strategic elements  that  both countries have in common aren't  there absolutely and  there it's really all about china yeah i  mean of course this has been the year  uh where we've seen more and more talk  about  uh a kind of new cold war between the  united states and china that china's  rise is a fundamental kind of threat to  american hegemony particularly in the  in the pacific region and  this is also a threat to india  and we've seen that really explode this  year with  border clashes between india and china  on the very remote  border that they have up high up in the  himalayan mountains  dozens of soldiers killed there's a  simmering long-standing border dispute  there  which has never been resolved but it  hasn't really flared up like this  of in a very long time and this is just  evidence of of  of the fact that both of the both of  these countries the rise of china is a  major challenge  uh so whoever wins the white house  building on that relationship with india  is going to be  a big priority i think for biden as well  and not just even for the united states  but other  western countries and other democracies  more broadly we also see even germany  talking about  an indo-pacific strategy trying to kind  of broaden  uh its uh its uh network of partners  in the indo-pacific region  so india's relations with the us and the  west something to watch  richard zever great to talk to you look  forward to hearing more about this  moving forward richard walker thanks so  much for coming in
 

